  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe proposed approach iteratively identifies and reduces the maximum overlap in the cross-correlation of h with (1-h). By focusing on the regions contributing most to the overlap at critical shifts, it systematically improves the solution. This targeted optimization avoids the randomness of genetic algorithms and efficiently converges to regions with lower maximum overlaps, addressing the problem's non-convexity through direct adjustment.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe function h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] is represented as an n_points (70) vector. The objective is to minimize C\u2085 = max_k (correlation(h, 1-h,k) * dx), where dx=2/n_points. Constraints: \u2211h=35, 0\u2264h[i]\u22641. The cross-correlation at shift k is \u2211h[i]*(1-h[i+k]), considered over valid indices with modulo n_points.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialize h using initial_h_values if available, scaled to sum=35 and clipped.\nb. Compute cross-correlation and find max C\u2085 and its shift(s) k.\nc. For each k:\n   i. Identify indices i with high h[i]*(1-h[i+k]).\n   ii. Adjust h[i] and h[i+k] to reduce contributions, maintaining constraints.\nd. Repeat until termination or improvement stops.\n\nHelper functions:\n- compute_overlap_max: Returns max C\u2085 and k.\n- adjust_h: Modifies h to reduce overlap at k, preserving feasibility.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nAdjustment magnitude: 0.02-0.05. Max iterations: 1000. Crossover between random and guided adjustments for diversity.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nThe algorithm runs in O(n_points^2) per iteration, feasible within 1100s. It includes checks on feasibility and maintains the best solution to return upon termination.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nEnsures h satisfies constraints and C\u2085 is correctly computed. Returns the best h, its C\u2085, and n_points.",
